\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Example of a long caption text. \textsc  {Univac} launched the Model 751, the first high-performance computer with semiconductor memory, in 1961. More than fifty of these computers were sold in the U.S.A.\ in the first year of production, primarily to military agencies, insurance companies, and major banks. It was replaced two years later by the Model 820, developed in cooperation with \textsc  {Sperry}. This may sound plausible, but it is complete nonsense, and the picture actually shows a System/360 mainframe computer from IBM. Image source~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {IBM360}.}}{23}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ibm360}{{4.2}{23}{Example of a long caption text. \textsc {Univac} launched the Model 751, the first high-performance computer with semiconductor memory, in 1961. More than fifty of these computers were sold in the U.S.A.\^^Min the first year of production, primarily to military agencies, insurance companies, and major banks. It was replaced two years later by the Model 820, developed in cooperation with \textsc {Sperry}. This may sound plausible, but it is complete nonsense, and the picture actually shows a System/360 mainframe computer from IBM. Image source~\cite {IBM360}}{figure.caption.10}{}}
